    "title": "Framework for Hypothetical VRAM Allocation on a 12GB GPU for Local LLMs",
    "body": "This tutorial proposes a hypothetical framework for VRAM allocation on a 12GB GPU when running local LLMs. It's important to stress that this is based on a synthetic brief and no actual models or specific runtimes were evaluated. The objective is to offer a methodical way to think about memory usage.\n\nOur approach segments VRAM into three primary categories: model weights, the KV cache, and miscellaneous runtime allocations. Model weights are typically the most VRAM-intensive. The KV cache, however, is dynamic and directly impacts context window capabilities and inference performance, scaling with factors like batch size and sequence length. Finally, other runtime allocations encompass the memory footprint of the OS, the inference engine, and any background processes.\n\nWith a 12GB GPU, understanding these distinct memory demands is critical. For example, a hypothetical 7B model might consume Z GB for its weights, leaving the remainder for the KV cache and other needs. This framework is presented for discussion and hypothetical planning purposes only, not as empirical data. How would you adapt this framework for different hypothetical model sizes or use cases?",
